---
/**
 * /fork-or-sweep/: when to run a dispersion sweep and when to fork a paused run.
 *
 * Both ship and the shipped examples compose them, so the page does not argue that
 * the fork replaces the ensemble. The retropropulsion table's `dv m/s` and `dv frozen`
 * columns print the coupled and the frozen-drag answer to one intervention.
 *
 * Sources: weather/main.rs and output.txt, corridor/main.rs, constants.rs and
 * output.txt, retropropulsion/output.txt and model.rs, shared/stages.rs (AxialWitness).
 */
import PageShell from '../../components/nav/PageShell.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import { MACHINE } from '../../consts';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'When to fork and when to sweep | DeepCausality CFD';
const description =
  'Sweep when the alternatives differ by an input chosen before the run. Fork when they must share a past. Both ship here, and the retropropulsion table prints the difference as two columns.';

/* weather/main.rs:72-84, condensed. */
const ensemble = `let table = CfdFlow::study("weather-dispersion table")
    .cases(model::weather_cases())
    .baseline(model::standard_day)      // the validated origin, built once
    .alternate(model::weather_world)    // six counterfactual atmospheres, each marked
    .ensemble(constants::MC_DRAWS)      // deterministic receiver-noise draws
    .couple(|case, draw| world::corridor_coupling(model::bias_departure(case.d_temp), draw))
    .march_for(constants::STEPS, world::initial_field)   // from a fresh initial field
    .reduce_ensemble(model::world_row)  // draw sets collapse to mean / scatter / worst
    .gates(model::weather_gates())
    .verdict()?;`;

/* corridor/main.rs:98-115, condensed. */
const fork = `let corridor = CfdFlow::study("bank-angle corridor")
    .cases(model::coarse_commands())
    .fork(&onset)                       // one paused, marched, coupled state
    .branch(model::bank_world)          // one world per bank command
    .continue_for(constants::BRANCH_STEPS)
    .reduce_all(model::score_branches)
    .gates(model::corridor_gates())
    .verdict()?;`;

/* retropropulsion/output.txt:24-29, verbatim. */
const roster = `  branch         cmd    flown  preserved  axial m/s2    prop kg     dv m/s  dv frozen
  coast         0.00   0.0000          —     10.5950       1.81    139.519      0.144
  low           0.20   0.2000     0.2510      7.4718      62.05     88.399     49.490
  mid           0.40   0.4000     0.1238      9.9667     122.30    120.335     99.984
  high          0.60   0.6000    -0.0161     12.8932     182.54    151.991    151.413
  hard          0.85   0.7931    -0.0611     17.1977     256.80    212.085    216.209`;

const compare = [
  {
    q: 'What varies',
    sweep: 'An input, chosen before the run starts.',
    forkc: 'The command or description each branch continues with, at a pause the flow chose.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What each branch starts from',
    sweep: 'A fresh initial field. Branches share a description, not a past.',
    forkc: 'The marched coupled field as it stands. Branches share a past.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What it answers',
    sweep: 'How wide is the outcome, given what I do not know?',
    forkc: 'Which command should I commit, given where the vehicle actually is?',
  },
  {
    q: 'What it costs',
    sweep: 'One full march per case per draw.',
    forkc: 'One march to the pause, then one continuation per branch.',
  },
];

const columns = [
  { name: 'branch', text: 'One alternative, named for its throttle.' },
  { name: 'cmd', text: 'The throttle the branch commands, as a fraction of full thrust: 0.00 is engine off, 1.00 is full thrust.' },
  { name: 'flown', text: 'The throttle the vehicle flew. The safety envelope clamps commands that would push the thrust coefficient past its limit: the hard branch commands 0.85 and flies 0.7931.' },
  { name: 'preserved', text: 'The fraction of the drag that survives the exhaust plume. It comes from the Jarvinen-Adams correlation at the branch’s thrust coefficient. A dash means the branch has no plume and the closure applies nothing. A negative value is the correlation’s wake-type branch, where the forebody force has changed sign.' },
  { name: 'axial m/s2', text: 'The deceleration along the flight path at the end of the continuation, read from the summed thrust and drag force.' },
  { name: 'prop kg', text: 'The propellant the branch burned over the continuation.' },
  { name: 'dv m/s', text: 'The velocity the branch shed over the continuation.' },
  { name: 'dv frozen', text: 'The velocity change over the same continuation with the drag closure held at its value from the fork. The gap to dv m/s is what the closure’s response to each throttle adds.' },
];
---
<PageShell title={title} description={description} path="/fork-or-sweep/">
  <header class="doc-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Fork or sweep</p>
    <h1>When to fork and when to sweep</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Sweep when the alternatives differ by an input chosen before the run. Fork when the
      alternatives must share a past.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="difference">
    <h2>How does a fork differ from a sweep?</h2>
    <p>
      A dispersion sweep (a Monte Carlo campaign over uncertain inputs) runs the whole model once
      per case and per noise draw. Every run starts from a fresh initial field, so the runs share a
      description and no past. A fork pauses one marched run when a condition on the flow becomes
      true and continues every branch from that state. An intervention feeds back into the flow its
      branch inherited.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col"></th>
            <th scope="col">Dispersion sweep</th>
            <th scope="col">State fork</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {compare.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{r.q}</th>
              <td data-label="Dispersion sweep">{r.sweep}</td>
              <td data-label="State fork">{r.forkc}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Both ship, in the same study language. The next two sections work one shipped example of
      each.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="sweep">
    <h2>A sweep measures how wide the outcome gets</h2>
    <p>
      The weather example flies six atmospheres, from polar winter to a hot day, and measures how
      far navigation drift through blackout spreads. Blackout is the stretch where the plasma
      around the vehicle cuts the satellite link. Polar-winter mean drift is 58.71 m against 41.60
      m on a standard day: the vehicle’s inertial navigation unit does not behave as assumed in
      the cold.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each row carries a scatter from eight noise draws. The gate (a pass or fail check on the
      table) can therefore require the separation to clear two combined standard deviations before
      it counts. It clears 5.7.
    </p>
    <p>
      Six atmospheres, each declared as one departure from a validated baseline, times eight draws
      makes 48 full descents. Each starts from a fresh initial field and flies all 850 coupled
      steps.
    </p>
    <Code code={ensemble} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />
    <p>
      The draws are deterministic low-discrepancy sequences, not a random number generator. The
      error bars are bit-reproducible, so a statistical gate can bind.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="fork">
    <h2>A fork chooses among alternatives from one state</h2>
    <p>
      The corridor example asks which bank angle to fly once blackout starts. The bank angle is the
      roll that steers the vehicle’s lift sideways. One march runs 131 coupled steps to the pause,
      at 73.2 km, and stops with the flow, the plasma sheath and the navigation filter as they
      stand. Seventeen candidate angles continue from that state for 100 steps each: six coarse,
      then eleven refined around the winner. The best, 11.5°, lands 2.07 m from the aim point.
    </p>
    <p>
      The whole corridor run takes 44.4 s on a 32×32 grid ({MACHINE}). That covers the march to the
      pause, both rounds of branches and three legs flown after them.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <p class="callout-title">A setup value, not a result</p>
      <p>
        The example places the aim point 20 m to the side of where the unsteered vehicle lands
        (<code>AIM_CROSS_RANGE_M</code> in its constants). The zero-bank miss of 20.0 m is
        therefore set by construction. The fork measures how close each candidate angle gets from
        the shared state.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      The grammar matches the sweep’s. Only the origin changes: <code>.fork(&amp;onset)</code>
      takes a paused run where <code>.baseline(...)</code> took a description. The cases, the
      concurrency, the scoring and the gates are shared.
    </p>
    <Code code={fork} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="coupling">
    <h2>Does the coupling change the answer?</h2>
    <p>
      In the retropropulsion example it does, and the run prints both answers. Retropropulsion fires an
      engine against the flow to slow a descent. The example forks the burning vehicle at mid-burn
      into five throttle branches. Each branch continues for a fixed number of steps, and the table
      reports what the branch did next to what a frozen-drag prediction says it would have done.
    </p>
    <p>The columns, left to right:</p>
    <dl class="deflist">
      {columns.map((c) => (
        <div>
          <dt><code>{c.name}</code></dt>
          <dd>{c.text}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
    <Code code={roster} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} />
    <p>
      On the low branch the run sheds 88.399 m/s where the frozen prediction says 49.490. Net
      deceleration is not monotone in throttle: it bottoms out on that branch at 7.4718 m/s2,
      below the 10.5950 m/s2 of coasting. Preserved drag falls through zero as the throttle rises.
      The coast branch shows the widest gap, 139.519 m/s against 0.144. With no plume the closure
      applies nothing, while the frozen column holds drag at its fork value. A campaign that fixes
      drag at its fork value reads the frozen column.
    </p>
    <div class="callout callout-warn">
      <p class="callout-title">Where the drag change comes from</p>
      <p>
        The preserved-drag column is the cited Jarvinen-Adams correlation, evaluated at each
        branch’s thrust coefficient (thrust over dynamic pressure times reference area). The
        simulated plume does not produce it. A study that coupled a momentum-carrying plume into the
        compressible layer did not reproduce the measured drag collapse
        (<a href="/boundaries/#srp">boundaries: retropropulsion drag</a>), so the marched plume
        supplies flow state and nothing more. The table shows that a drag closure responding to
        each branch’s throttle changes the answer. It does not show that the simulated plume
        produces the collapse.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="compose">
    <h2>Sweeps and forks compose</h2>
    <p>
      A single run cannot separate a mechanism from a draw, and no amount of forking fixes that.
      An earlier single-draw weather table showed the cold-day row eight percent below its
      predicted value, which read as a physics finding. The Monte Carlo mean identified it as one
      draw.
    </p>
    <p>
      The shipped examples therefore use both. The weather table sizes a dispersion margin across
      48 descents. The retropropulsion descent reads that table in flight and forks against it. Its
      gate 5 measures what the composition bought: informed and uninformed guidance started the
      landing burn 14.06 m apart in altitude (139.12 m against 125.06 m), and the informed
      guidance spent 7.28 kg more propellant. That is the dispersion-sized reserve, measured
      rather than configured.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="limits">
    <h2>What a fork does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A fork varies the world configuration. A what-if about what the guidance believed had to
        run as two separate marches, and it carries no <code>!!ContextAlternation!!</code> marker.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <code>fork</code> / <code>branch</code> / <code>continue_for</code> chain does not
        thread the audit sink, so it writes no log files. Each branch carries its event log in the
        returned report.
      </li>
      <li>
        The corridor is the weakest evidence for the coupling: across its bank branches the flow
        observables agree to three digits. The retropropulsion table is where branches change the
        flow.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href="/blueprints/fork-a-running-simulation/">Blueprint: fork a running simulation</a>
      <a href="/tutorial/stage-2-weather/">Walk 2: one baseline, six worlds</a>
      <a href="/boundaries/">Measured boundaries</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</PageShell>

<style>
  .doc-sec :global(pre.astro-code) { max-width: none; }

  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--t-body-sm); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: var(--space-3); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1); vertical-align: top; }
  thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  tbody th { font-weight: 400; color: var(--fg-0); }
  tbody td { color: var(--fg-1); }
  tbody tr:last-child th, tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }

  .callout p { color: var(--fg-1); }
</style>
